From: Arch POWER
Subject: [PATCH] kbuild: let pacman-pkg override CARCH

scripts/Makefile.package hardcodes CARCH="$(UTS_MACHINE)" when invoking
makepkg. UTS_MACHINE is the kernel's own name for the architecture --
"ppc64le" here -- but a distribution's CARCH is whatever that distribution
tags its packages with, and Arch POWER uses "powerpc64le".

The mismatch is not cosmetic: pacman refuses the resulting package with
ALPM_ERR_PKG_INVALID_ARCH, so "make pacman-pkg" produces a kernel that
cannot be installed on the distribution it was built on.

Overriding UTS_MACHINE instead would work but is wrong -- it changes the
kernel's own uname and forces a relink. PACMAN_CARCH affects only the
packaging step.

  make pacman-pkg PACMAN_CARCH=powerpc64le PACMAN_PKGBASE=linux-power9

--- a/scripts/Makefile.package
+++ b/scripts/Makefile.package
@@ -153,7 +153,7 @@
 pacman-pkg:
 	@ln -srf $(srctree)/scripts/package/PKGBUILD $(objtree)/PKGBUILD
 	+BUILDDIR="$(realpath $(objtree))/pacman" \
-		CARCH="$(UTS_MACHINE)" \
+		CARCH="$(if $(PACMAN_CARCH),$(PACMAN_CARCH),$(UTS_MACHINE))" \
 		KBUILD_MAKEFLAGS="$(MAKEFLAGS)" \
 		KBUILD_REVISION="$(shell $(srctree)/scripts/build-version)" \
 		makepkg $(MAKEPKGOPTS)
